# Hook scripts are Authenticode-signed at publish time; signatures are never
# committed.
#
# An Authenticode signature covers the file's exact bytes, so a checkout that
# rewrites line endings produces a file whose signature no longer verifies. The
# signing runner is Windows, where a clone with `core.autocrlf=true` would
# convert LF to CRLF before signing — the signature would then cover content
# that no Linux consumer ever receives, and the published package would carry a
# signature valid for nobody.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, so every checkout
# on every platform sees the bytes exactly as committed. Scoped to the hook
# scripts rather than set repo-wide: a blanket `* text=auto` would renormalize
# every tracked file in the repository.
hooks/*.ps1 -text
hooks/*.sh  -text
